my name is Deirdre Guan and I just  founded the center for humans and  machines at the Max Planck Institute for  human development in Burley  the discipline of artificial  intelligence which is where I started my  study is an engineering discipline it  has the goal of building intelligent  machines so the idea is you look at  other intelligent behavior in the world  like human intelligence or animal  intelligence and then you try to mimic  that by engineering clever algorithms  having algorithms that learn on their  own and so on but your goal is always to  engineer a particular behavior that you  have in mind before the discipline of  machine behavior is the other side of  this coin once the machines have been  engineered and deployed how do you then  observe them in the real world and how  they interact with each other with  humans individual humans as well as  groups of humans so machine behavior is  a discipline of the behavioral sciences  and one should be able to study machine  behavior without understanding  necessarily how those machines are built  a machine could be designed to achieve  one goal but then once you deploy it  start doing other things they may be  because we cannot anticipate all of the  effects of this algorithm on the news or  on misinformation online or what have  you  and I think it's a behavioral scientists  have a lot to offer  to help us understand these entities  especially now that they are very much  permeating our world we carry algorithm  to pay our algorithms with us with our  cell phones they serve us news they  recommend music and products to us they  even suggest things for us to say to our  friends and so on so I think it's  imperative to bring in the behavioral  sciences so our change in behavior is  really an invitation to people from  other disciplines outside of computer  science to help us understand the things  that we are building  ten years from now it would be amazing  if we had some kind of broad theory of  different kinds of behaviors that  machines can exhibit we have a fairly  sophisticated theory of human behavior  we understand human decision-making even  errors when humans can be rational or  irrational what kind of mistakes they  make and so on but we don't have a  similar theory of machines it would be  nice if we could have a kind of  systematic way of saying there are three  types of autonomous vehicle behavior or  there are five types of recommender  system behavior it doesn't matter how  these systems learn how they're  engineered but they somehow exhibit  these kind of classes or types of  behavior so we're kind of in a stage  where we're building a broader theory of  all of this and nothing is the lacking
 

